The state parameter is **URL-encoded** in the authorizationUrl, so the `&` becomes `%26` and `=` becomes `%3D` — the parameter boundary attack is **blocked** by proper URL encoding.
[0m$ [0mcurl -s -m15 -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" -H "Origin: https://evil.example.com" -d '{"domain":"spare.com","state":"x&redirect_uri=https://evil.example.com/callback"}' https://api.sparelabs.com/v1/identity/workos/auth | python3 -c "
import sys,json
d=json.load(sys.stdin)
url=d.get('authorizationUrl','')
# Check if the & is encoded
has_encoded_amp = '%26' in url
has_raw_amp = '&redirect_uri=evil' in url
print('Encoded & found:', has_encoded_amp)
print('Raw & with redirect_uri:', has_raw_amp)
print('INJECTION BLOCKED: YES' if has_encoded_amp and not has_raw_amp else 'INJECTION BLOCKED: NO')
"
Encoded & found: True
Raw & with redirect_uri: False
INJECTION BLOCKED: YES
[0m
**Result: redirect_uri injection BLOCKED** — Spare properly URL-encodes the state parameter in the authorizationUrl, preventing parameter boundary attacks.

The state reflection is still usable for phishing (attacker controls the value users see), but cannot inject OAuth parameters like `redirect_uri`. Downgrading this hypothesis from 62 → **40** (just above threshold).

